From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Connor Prussin <connor@prussin.net>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:43:51 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: embed a brokered surface in the page

Step 3 of the spike in docs/architecture/ENGINE-FORK.md. Moves the embedder
from the browser's own window into Blink: canvas.embedExternalSurface() points
the cc::SurfaceLayer HTMLCanvasElement already owns at a viz surface the
browser brokered to a producer that is not a renderer.

Not killed. The canvas does not refuse a surface it did not allocate, and
nothing under it does either: SurfaceLayerBridge::EmbedSurface() takes a
SurfaceId and never compares it to its own frame_sink_id_, and
cc::SurfaceLayer::SetSurfaceId() stores a SurfaceRange without looking at the
FrameSinkId's namespace.

The two halves of the SurfaceId come from opposite sides, which is
RemoteFrame's split. The browser owns the FrameSinkId because a brokered id is
in its own namespace, client id 0, and EmbeddedFrameSinkProvider rejects any id
whose client id is not the calling renderer's. The page owns the
LocalSurfaceId because it is the embedder, and the embed_token in it is the
capability the producer needs.

The renderer gets ExternalSurfaceProvider, which is one method wide and can
only grant. A FrameSinkBroker pipe is unrestricted authority to allocate frame
sinks in viz and stays reachable only over the named socket.

Deletes step 2's stand-in embedder and, with it, the call in
browser_main_loop.cc: the broker and its socket are now created when a page
first asks to embed, and Embed()'s reply is held until a producer connects. An
<app> element exists before the window behind it does.

Edits seven files Chromium owns, five of them Blink's. The design doc's
"roughly four" was optimistic and is corrected there.
